
Learn how to record real API interactions from a Go application and replay them as automated tests using Keploy.


<div className="info-box">

  <strong>What you'll build:</strong>

  <div>

    A simple Go URL-shortener API backed by PostgreSQL, with Keploy recording

    and replaying its HTTP interactions.

  </div>

</div>


## What you'll learn


By the end of this tutorial, you'll know how to:


- Start a Go application with PostgreSQL

- Verify that the API is working

- Record real API interactions with Keploy

- Generate HTTP test cases and PostgreSQL mocks

- Replay recorded test cases

- Inspect Keploy-generated test artifacts

- Understand what Keploy is doing behind the scenes


---


<h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>


Before starting, make sure you have:


- Go installed

- Docker installed and running

- WSL/Linux environment

- Keploy installed

- `curl` available

- The Go sample application available locally


<div className="info-box">

  <strong>Why Keploy?</strong>

  <div>

    Instead of manually writing every integration test, Keploy can capture

    real API interactions and turn them into reusable test cases and mocks.

  </div>

</div>


---


<h2 id="start-postgresql">Step 1 — Start PostgreSQL</h2>


The sample Go application uses PostgreSQL as its database.


Move into the Go sample application:


```bash

cd ~/samples-go/echo-sql

```


If PostgreSQL is not already running, start it with:


```bash

docker compose up -d postgres

```


You can verify the container:


```bash

docker compose ps

```


You should see the PostgreSQL container running and healthy.


<div className="note-box">

  <strong>Already running?</strong>

  <div>

    If PostgreSQL is already running in another terminal, do not start another

    PostgreSQL container. Leave the existing container running and continue.

  </div>

</div>


---


<h2 id="verify-api">Step 2 — Verify the API</h2>


Before using Keploy, make sure the Go application itself works.


Open a terminal and move to the sample application:


```bash

cd ~/samples-go/echo-sql

```


Start the Go application:


```bash

go run .

```


The application should connect to PostgreSQL and start the HTTP server.


For this tutorial, the API is available on port `8082`.


Open a second terminal and test the URL endpoint:


```bash

curl --request POST \

  --url http://localhost:8082/url \

  --header 'content-type: application/json' \

  --data '{"url":"https://github.com"}'

```


A successful response should contain a generated shortened URL.


For example:


```json

{

  "ts": 1716546241744809281,

  "url": "http://localhost:8082/Lhr4BWAi"

}

```


The exact timestamp and generated short URL will be different for every request.


You can also test the root endpoint:


```bash

curl http://localhost:8082/

```


<div className="success-box">

  <strong>Checkpoint</strong>

  <div>

    If the curl request receives an HTTP response, the Go application and

    PostgreSQL connection are working. Continue to the Keploy recording step.

  </div>

</div>


---


<h2 id="record">Step 3 — Record with Keploy</h2>


Now we will let Keploy observe the application's real HTTP and database interactions.


Keep the Go application running in one terminal.


Open another terminal and move into the project:


```bash

cd ~/samples-go/echo-sql

```


Start the application through Keploy recording:


```bash

keploy record -c "go run ."

```


Keploy will start its agent and proxy.


You may see messages similar to:


```text

starting TCP DNS server

Keploy has taken control of the DNS resolution mechanism

Proxy started at port 16789

Starting Agent's HTTP server

```


These messages indicate that Keploy has started its recording infrastructure.


With the application running under Keploy, send API requests from another terminal.


For example:


```bash

curl --request POST \

  --url http://localhost:8082/url \

  --header 'content-type: application/json' \

  --data '{"url":"https://github.com"}'

```


You can create more than one interaction:


```bash

curl --request POST \

  --url http://localhost:8082/url \

  --header 'content-type: application/json' \

  --data '{"url":"https://google.com"}'

```


Keploy captures these interactions and stores them under the project's `keploy`

directory.


After recording, inspect the generated files:


```bash

find keploy -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort

```


Typical generated artifacts include:


```text

keploy/

├── test-set-1/

│   ├── config.yaml

│   ├── mappings.yaml

│   ├── mocks.yaml

│   └── tests/

│       ├── post-url-1.yaml

│       └── get-root-1.yaml

├── test-set-2/

│   ├── config.yaml

│   ├── mappings.yaml

│   ├── mocks.yaml

│   └── tests/

└── reports/

```


The exact files and test-set numbers can differ depending on previous recording

and replay runs.


---


<h2 id="test">Step 4 — Replay the tests</h2>


Once Keploy has generated test cases and mocks, we can replay them.


First, make sure the application is available for the test run as required by

your Keploy command.


From the project directory:


```bash

cd ~/samples-go/echo-sql

```


Run the recorded tests:


```bash

keploy test -c "go run ."

```


Keploy will start the application, replay the recorded requests, use the

generated mocks where required, and compare the resulting responses with the

recorded responses.


A successful run looks similar to:


```text

TESTRUN SUMMARY. For test-set: "test-set-2"


Total tests:       2

Total test passed: 2

Total test failed: 0

```


For a complete run, you may see output similar to:


```text

COMPLETE TESTRUN SUMMARY.


Total tests:       6

Total test passed: 6

Total test failed: 0

Total time taken:  "5.10 s"

```


This means the recorded test cases were replayed successfully.


---


<h2 id="inspect">Step 5 — Inspect the generated artifacts</h2>


Keploy stores test cases, mocks, mappings, and reports inside the `keploy`

directory.


List the generated files:


```bash

find keploy -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort

```


Inspect a generated test case:


```bash

cat keploy/test-set-1/tests/post-url-1.yaml

```


Inspect the generated PostgreSQL mocks:


```bash

cat keploy/test-set-1/mocks.yaml

```


Inspect a test report:


```bash

cat keploy/reports/test-run-0/test-set-1-report.yaml

```


The test YAML contains information about the HTTP request and expected response,

while `mocks.yaml` contains recorded external/database interactions used during

replay.


---


<h2 id="coverage">Step 6 — Understand the coverage report</h2>


After a successful Keploy test run, Keploy can calculate coverage for the run.


You may see output such as:


```text

Total Coverage Percentage: 58.82%

```


Coverage represents the portion of the application's relevant execution that

was exercised by the recorded tests. It is useful for understanding how much of

the application is being exercised, but it should not be interpreted as proof

that every possible behavior is tested.


Coverage reports are stored under:


```bash

keploy/reports/

```


You can inspect them with:


```bash

find keploy/reports -type f | sort

```


---


<h2 id="behind-the-scenes">Step 7 — Behind the scenes</h2>


The complete workflow can be understood as:


```text

Real API request

       |

       v

Go application

       |

       +------> PostgreSQL

       |

       v

Keploy agent / proxy

       |

       +------> Records HTTP interaction

       |

       +------> Captures database interaction

       |

       v

Test cases + mocks

       |

       v

Keploy test replay

       |

       v

Compare actual response with recorded response

       |

       v

Pass / Fail + report

```


### What happens during recording?


When you run the application through Keploy and send an API request:


1. The request reaches the Go application through Keploy's proxy.

2. The application processes the request.

3. The application communicates with PostgreSQL.

4. Keploy observes the interaction.

5. Keploy creates a reusable HTTP test case.

6. Database interactions can be stored as mocks.

7. The generated artifacts are written into the `keploy` directory.


### What happens during replay?


During a test run:


1. Keploy starts the application.

2. A previously recorded HTTP request is replayed.

3. Keploy provides the recorded mock interactions when required.

4. The application processes the request.

5. Keploy receives the application's response.

6. The response is compared with the recorded response.

7. The test is marked as passed or failed.

8. Keploy generates a test report and coverage information.


---


<h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>


### PostgreSQL is already running


If Docker shows the PostgreSQL container as running and healthy, do not start

another copy.


Check it with:


```bash

docker compose ps

```


### curl says "Could not connect to server"


For example:


```text

curl: (7) Failed to connect to localhost port 8082

```


This means nothing is currently listening on port `8082`.


Start the Go application, or start it through Keploy if you are recording or

testing.


### Keploy reports an unknown mock type


If you see:


```text

failed to unmarshal a mock yaml doc of unknown type

{"type":"Postgres"}

```


inspect the generated mock file:


```bash

cat keploy/test-set-1/mocks.yaml

```


Do not immediately delete all test data. First identify which test-set and mock

file contains the incompatible entry.


If you are intentionally regenerating the test artifacts, remove only the

specific problematic test-set and record again.


### Check the current Keploy artifacts


Use:


```bash

find keploy -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort

```


This is safer than assuming a particular test-set number because Keploy can

create multiple test sets and reports during repeated runs.


---


<h2 id="complete">Tutorial complete</h2>


You have now followed the complete flow:


```text

PostgreSQL

   ↓

Go application

   ↓

Verify API with curl

   ↓

Record with Keploy

   ↓

Generate test cases and mocks

   ↓

Replay tests

   ↓

Generate reports and coverage

```


The key idea is simple: Keploy turns real application interactions into

replayable automated tests, including the required mocked interactions, so the

same behavior can be tested repeatedly without manually writing every

integration test.
